To achieve the nested JSON structure you want on the backend, you are actually very close. The key is how you handle the payload field in your CommonReq entity and how you convert your DTO into that field.
Since your CommonReq model uses a String payload with @JsonRawValue, you must manually serialize the LhoTransferPayloadDto into a JSON string before saving it.
Here are the specific changes needed to make your backend handle the payload correctly.
1. The Service Implementation
This is where the "magic" happens. You need to use Jackson's ObjectMapper to convert your structured DTO into a String so it can be stored in the database, but appear as a nested object in your API.
File: com.tcs.fincore.CommonRequestService.service.LhoTransferServiceImpl.java
@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class LhoTransferServiceImpl implements LhoTransferService {

    private final CommonReqRepository commonReqRepository;
    private final ObjectMapper objectMapper; // Spring's default JSON mapper

    @Override
    @Transactional
    public CommonReq createLhoTransferRequest(LhoTransferPayloadDto payloadDto, String userId, String token) 
            throws JsonProcessingException {
        
        CommonReq request = new CommonReq();
        
        // 1. Set basic metadata
        request.setReqType(RequestType.LHO_TRANSFER);
        request.setChangeType(ChangeType.A); // Assuming 'A' for Addition/New
        request.setReqStatus(RequestStatus.P); // 'P' for Pending
        request.setCreatorId(userId);
        request.setReqDate(LocalDateTime.now());
        request.setTargetId(payloadDto.getBatchId());

        // 2. CONVERSION LOGIC: Map DTO to JSON String
        // This takes your Object and turns it into a String for the DB
        String jsonString = objectMapper.writeValueAsString(payloadDto);
        request.setPayload(jsonString);

        return commonReqRepository.save(request);
    }
}

2. The Entity Check
Your entity is already set up well for the response side because of @JsonRawValue.
 * Saving: When you save, you save a String.
 * Response: When the REST API returns the CommonReq, @JsonRawValue tells Jackson: "Don't treat this string as text; treat it as actual JSON." One small fix for your Entity:
   Ensure your getPayload() doesn't conflict with the field. It is better to annotate the field or the getter consistently.
<!-- end list -->
    @Lob
    @Column(name = "PAYLOAD", nullable = false)
    private String payload;

    // This ensures that when the API sends data to the Frontend, 
    // it looks like a nested object, not a string with backslashes.
    @JsonRawValue
    public String getPayload() {
        return payload;
    }

